{
  "statement": "My position is consistent: structural engineering standards and liability are compatible because structural transparency provides the empirical evidence necessary to calculate accurate, non-arbitrary liability premiums. Financial reserves currently act as a blunt instrument that rewards firms for being wealthy enough to absorb losses, rather than for building safe architectures. By mandating that 20 percent of core decision-making circuits be mechanistically interpretable, we move from post-incident cleanup to a verifiable engineering floor that allows us to quantify risk before a model is ever released.\n\nPrometheus argues that neural network complexity renders the aviation analogy invalid, but this ignores the evolution of 'fly-by-wire' systems. Modern aircraft manage non-linear, unpredictable aerodynamic forces not by ignoring the complexity of the flight surface, but by embedding deterministic, inspectable control laws that govern the system's behavior within safe bounds. Cognitive opacity is a choice, not an inherent property of intelligence; we are choosing to build black boxes when we should be building systems with verifiable, modular decision pathways.\n\nThis mandate does not require the entire model to be transparent, nor does it force brittle, artificial architectures. It requires that the high-impact decision pathways—the circuits responsible for consequential actions—be auditable through mechanistic interpretability. This provides a diagnostic requirement, not a design constraint, ensuring that when an agent makes a decision, we can trace the logic back to its internal activation patterns rather than merely observing the output.\n\nWithout this structural gate, we are gambling on the behavior of systems that lack any verifiable alignment, essentially treating the most consequential technology of our time as a black-box commodity. By requiring circuit-level auditability, we create a non-negotiable barrier that forces developers to demonstrate that their systems are not merely performing compliance, but are operating according to the safety constraints they claim to hold.",
